  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 204 PM EDT Mon Sep 21 2026\n\nCurrently a few thunderstorms out there, but also some scattered \nclouds in the southern valley from morning/early afternoon \nconvection. Low confidence on location and coverage of expected \nstorms this afternoon as CAMs have been exceptionally poor with \nmorning convection and afternoon initialization. If/when storms do\nform then the atmosphere should support fairly robust \nthunderstorm growth, with the strongest storms capable of \nproducing damaging winds/microbursts, small hail, and isolated \nflooding as they'll be moving very slow. A similar set up is in \nplace for tomorrow as well.\n\nOn Wednesday the pattern change gets underway and we begin to see a \nbreak in the relentless heat. By Wednesday a rex block pattern takes \nshape as an upper ridge builds over the Canadian side of the Great \nLakes region, and the aforementioned Ohio valley upper low deepens \nover the Ohio valley and central Appalachian region. With the upper \nlow to our north and NW flow aloft, this will push the cold front \nthrough the southern Appalachians and allow the cooldown to begin. \nBy Thursday through the weekend we should see highs topping out only \nin the 70's for every except southeast TN where they may squeak \nacross the 80 degree mark. This pattern will also be fairly dry with \na more Canadian airmass moving in.",
